@article
    @key=geosetup
    @title=Enabling geospatial support
    @brief=Turning spatial support on in the database and in your project.
    @ingroup=tutorialsgeo

    [i]Geospatial support is available starting from version 1.9.7.[/i]

    The framework supports a spatial (geometry) column type across five engines: Microsoft SQL Server,
    Oracle (Locator), PostgreSQL (PostGIS), MySQL and SQLite (SpatiaLite). A geometry value travels between
    the application and the database as plain OGC Well-Known Binary (WKB, a [c]byte[][/c]); the framework
    owns no geometry object model. Spatial support is [b]disabled by default[/b] and must be turned on
    before any connection is opened. This article covers that one-time setup; the [link=geosql]SQL-level[/link]
    and [link=geoentities]entity-level[/link] articles then show how to use it.

    [b]On the database side[/b]

    Microsoft SQL Server, Oracle and MySQL need no client-side switch - their geometry types are built into
    the engine. PostgreSQL and SQLite require the engine's spatial component to be present:

    @list
        @list-item
            [b]PostgreSQL (PostGIS)[/b] - install the PostGIS extension in the target database once (as a
            superuser or the database owner):

            ```+sql
            CREATE EXTENSION postgis;
            ```
        @end
        @list-item
            [b]SQLite (SpatiaLite)[/b] - make the [c]mod_spatialite[/c] native library available. Either
            install it system-wide (Linux: [c]apt-get install libsqlite3-mod-spatialite[/c]; macOS:
            [c]brew install spatialite-tools[/c]; Windows: the [c]mod_spatialite[/c] DLL together with its
            dependent DLLs), or add the [c]Spatialite.Native[/c] NuGet package, which ships the native
            binaries for the common platforms.
        @end
    @end

    [b]In your project[/b]

    Turn spatial support on once, before opening any connection:

    @list
        @list-item
            PostgreSQL:

            ```+csharp
            PostgresDbConnectionFactory.EnableSpatial = true;
            ```

            When enabled, the driver checks that PostGIS is installed as the connection opens and fails fast
            if it is not.
        @end
        @list-item
            SQLite:

            ```+csharp
            SqliteGlobalOptions.EnableSpatial = true;
            // optional: an explicit library path or name (default "mod_spatialite")
            SqliteGlobalOptions.SpatialiteLibrary = "mod_spatialite";
            ```

            As a connection opens, the driver loads the extension and initializes the spatial metadata. For an
            in-memory SQLite database, keep a single long-lived connection for the whole application lifetime.
        @end
    @end

    [b]Working with geometry objects (optional)[/b]

    A geometry property can be a raw [c]byte[][/c] (WKB) with no extra dependency, or a geometry object (for
    example a NetTopologySuite geometry). The object form needs a registered
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.Geometry.IGeometryCodec]IGeometryCodec[/clink]; the shipped codec lives in the
    optional [c]Gehtsoft.EF.Geo.NetTopologySuite[/c] module and is installed once at start-up:

    ```+csharp
    NtsGeometry.Register();
    ```

    The framework then converts geometry objects to and from WKB transparently, so the core never depends on
    the geometry library. Raw [c]byte[][/c] properties need no codec.
@end


@article
    @key=geosql
    @title=Geospatial (geometry) support in SQL
    @brief=Storing and querying geometry columns through the SQL query builders.
    @ingroup=tutorialsgeo

    The framework supports a spatial (geometry) column type across five engines: Microsoft SQL Server,
    Oracle (Locator), PostgreSQL (PostGIS), MySQL and SQLite (SpatiaLite).

    [b]The data model[/b]

    A geometry value is always moved between the application and the database as plain OGC [b]Well-Known
    Binary[/b] (WKB), that is, a [c]byte[][/c]. The framework owns no geometry object model and no parser;
    the spatial reference identifier (SRID, default 4326) is carried on the column declaration, not inside
    the value. On the client side a geometry is therefore a [c]byte[][/c]; converting it to and from a
    geometry object (points, polygons, and so on) is done by an optional codec, described in the
    [link=geosetup]setup article[/link].

    A geometry column is described by [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.Metadata.GeometryColumnMetadata]GeometryColumnMetadata[/clink]
    (CLR type, SRID, [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.Geometry.GeometrySubtype]GeometrySubtype[/clink],
    Z/M dimensionality, nullability and the declared spatial indexes). The column type and any spatial index
    are emitted per engine by the table-creation builder.

    [b]Enabling spatial support[/b]

    Spatial support is disabled by default and must be turned on (and, for PostgreSQL and SQLite, the
    engine's spatial component installed) before any connection is opened - see the
    [link=geosetup]setup article[/link].

    [b]Creating a geometry column and a spatial index[/b]

    A geometry column and its spatial index are declared through the entity metadata (see the entity-level
    article) and emitted by the table-creation builder for the target engine. Bounding box and tolerance for
    the index are supplied on the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.SpatialIndexAttribute]SpatialIndex[/clink]
    declaration; Microsoft SQL Server and Oracle require the bounding box, the other engines ignore it.

    [b]Reading and writing values[/b]

    On the wire the direction decides the form:

    @list
        @list-item
            [b]Client to server[/b] is always WKB wrapped in the dialect constructor: the insert and update
            builders wrap a bound [c]byte[][/c] parameter in [c]ST_GeomFromWKB(@p, srid)[/c] (or the engine's
            equivalent) automatically for a geometry column.
        @end
        @list-item
            [b]Server to client[/b] depends on the destination. A value returned to the application is wrapped
            in the WKB output function ([c]ST_AsBinary[/c]) so it is portable; a value that stays on the server
            (for example, feeding another predicate as a sub-query) is projected in its native form. The two
            forms are selected by [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.GeometryValueForm]GeometryValueForm[/clink].
        @end
    @end

    [b]Predicates and functions[/b]

    Spatial operations are rendered per engine through two dedicated channels on the language specifics:
    the topological / within-distance predicates enumerated by
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.SqlGeoPredicateId]SqlGeoPredicateId[/clink]
    (Intersects, Contains, Within, Disjoint, Touches, Overlaps, Crosses, Equals and DWithin), and the
    measurement / accessor functions enumerated by
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.SqlGeoFunctionId]SqlGeoFunctionId[/clink]
    (Area, Length, Distance, X, Y, SRID, GeometryType, IsEmpty, Envelope, and the WKB construction / output
    functions).

    On the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.QueryBuilder.ConditionBuilder]ConditionBuilder[/clink] a complete
    spatial predicate is added with [c]Where.GeoPredicate(...)[/c], and a scalar used as a comparison operand
    with [c]Where.GeoScalar(...)[/c]. On the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.QueryBuilder.SelectQueryBuilder]SelectQueryBuilder[/clink] a geometry value is
    projected with [c]AddGeometryValueToResultset(...)[/c] and a scalar with
    [c]AddGeometryScalarToResultset / ToOrderBy / ToGroupBy(...)[/c] (order by distance gives a
    nearest-neighbour ordering).

    @example
        @title=Example: cities within a region and the nearest three to a point
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        ```
        // rows whose geometry intersects a bound region (WKB parameter "region")
        var b = connection.GetSelectQueryBuilder(cities);
        b.AddToResultset(cities["name"]);
        b.Where.GeoPredicate(SqlGeoPredicateId.Intersects, cities["shape"], "region");

        // the three nearest cities to a probe point (order by distance, take 3)
        var n = connection.GetSelectQueryBuilder(cities);
        n.AddToResultset(cities["name"]);
        n.AddGeometryScalarToResultset(SqlGeoFunctionId.Distance, cities["shape"], DbType.Double, "d", parameterName: "p");
        n.AddGeometryScalarToOrderBy(SqlGeoFunctionId.Distance, cities["shape"], SortDir.Asc, parameterName: "p");
        n.Limit = 3;
        ```
    @end

    [b]Per-engine notes and limitations[/b]

    @list
        @list-item
            Measurements are [b]planar[/b]. Distance, Area and Length on longitude/latitude data are computed
            in degrees, not metres; the geography type is out of scope.
        @end
        @list-item
            Oracle Locator cannot express the OGC [c]Crosses[/c] predicate nor a clean [c]IsEmpty[/c]; both
            throw a "feature not supported" error on Oracle and are available on the other four engines.
        @end
        @list-item
            MySQL stores only two-dimensional geometry; declaring Z or M is rejected on MySQL.
        @end
        @list-item
            Spatial aggregates (union / collect / extent), mass update of a geometry field and ordering or
            grouping by a raw (non-scalar) geometry value are intentionally not provided.
        @end
    @end
@end


@article
    @key=geoentities
    @title=Geospatial (geometry) support for entities
    @brief=Declaring, storing and querying geometry properties on entities and in LINQ.
    @ingroup=tutorialsgeo

    Entities can carry geometry properties that map onto the spatial column type described in the
    [link=geosql]SQL-level article[/link]. Spatial support must be enabled before the examples below will
    run - see the [link=geosetup]setup article[/link] (install the engine's spatial component and turn on
    the driver switch).

    [b]Declaring a geometry property[/b]

    Mark the property with [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.GeometryEntityPropertyAttribute]GeometryEntityProperty[/clink]
    instead of the ordinary entity-property attribute. The attribute carries the SRID (default 4326), the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.Geometry.GeometrySubtype]GeometrySubtype[/clink] (Point, Polygon,
    MultiLineString, ..., or the generic Geometry), the Z/M dimensionality and nullability. A repeatable
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.SpatialIndexAttribute]SpatialIndex[/clink] attribute declares a spatial index
    (with an optional bounding box and tolerance).

    A geometry property may be declared in one of two ways:

    @list
        @list-item
            As a [c]byte[][/c] - the raw WKB. No geometry library is needed; the application works with WKB
            bytes directly.
        @end
        @list-item
            As a geometry object (for example a NetTopologySuite geometry). This requires the optional
            geometry codec to be registered at start-up - see the [link=geosetup]setup article[/link]. The
            framework then converts the object to and from WKB transparently, so the core never depends on
            the geometry library.
        @end
    @end

    ```+csharp
    [Entity(Table = "city")]
    public class City
    {
        [EntityProperty(Field = "id", AutoId = true)] public int Id { get; set; }
        [EntityProperty(Field = "name")] public string Name { get; set; }

        // raw WKB form
        [GeometryEntityProperty(Field = "shape", Subtype = GeometrySubtype.Point)]
        public byte[] Shape { get; set; }
    }
    ```

    [b]Storing and reading[/b]

    Saving and updating an entity round-trips a geometry property with no extra code: the insert and update
    queries detect the geometry column and wrap the value in the dialect constructor automatically, and a
    [c]null[/c] geometry stores SQL NULL. Reading a whole entity back populates the property from the portable
    WKB output form, decoding it through the codec when the property is an object type.

    [b]Filtering, ordering and grouping[/b]

    The entity condition builder gains geometry members that resolve the property, bind the operand and render
    the predicate for the engine:

    @list
        @list-item
            [c]Where.GeoPredicateOf(name, op, operand, ...)[/c] adds a complete topological or within-distance
            predicate; the operand is a WKB [c]byte[][/c], a native-geometry sub-query, or - through the NTS
            module - a geometry object.
        @end
        @list-item
            [c]Where.GeoScalarOf(name, op, ...)[/c] exposes a measurement or accessor as a comparison operand
            (for example, area greater than a threshold).
        @end
    @end

    On the select side [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase]SelectEntitiesQueryBase[/clink]
    gains [c]AddGeometryToResultset(...)[/c] (choosing the Wkb or Native
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.GeometryValueForm]form[/clink]) and
    [c]AddGeometryScalarToResultset / ToOrderBy / ToGroupBy(...)[/c] for scalar projection, nearest-neighbour
    ordering and grouping. Whole-entity reads project a geometry column through the WKB output form
    automatically. A projection of a geometry scalar or aggregate returns a tuple, not the entity, so such a
    query is built on the free-form select rather than the whole-entity read.

    Mass delete by a spatial predicate is supported; mass update of a geometry field is not (see the
    limitations in the [link=geosql]SQL-level article[/link]).

    [b]LINQ[/b]

    Geometry is available in LINQ through the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.SqlSpatial]SqlSpatial[/clink]
    marker methods, usable in [c]Where[/c], [c]OrderBy[/c], [c]Select[/c] and [c]GroupBy[/c]. The first
    argument is the geometry property; the operand is supplied as WKB [c]byte[][/c] (encode a geometry object
    at the call site with the codec, [c]codec.ToWkb(geometry, false)[/c]), which keeps the query core free of
    any geometry-library dependency.

    @example
        @title=Example: entity query and the equivalent LINQ
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        ```
        byte[] region = codec.ToWkb(regionPolygon, false);

        // entity query API
        using (var q = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase<City>())
        {
            q.AddToResultset("Name", "name");
            q.Where.GeoPredicateOf<City>("Shape", SqlGeoPredicateId.Intersects, region);
            q.ExecuteReader();
            while (q.ReadNext())
                names.Add(q.GetValue<string>("name"));
        }

        // LINQ
        var names = connection.GetCollectionOf<City>()
            .Where(c => SqlSpatial.Intersects(c.Shape, region))
            .OrderBy(c => SqlSpatial.Distance(c.Shape, region))
            .Select(c => c.Name)
            .ToList();
        ```
    @end

    LINQ ordering in this framework is ascending only, and the same limitations as the SQL layer apply
    (planar measurements, Oracle without Crosses / IsEmpty, MySQL two-dimensional only).
@end
